>student|Lab 1 for my microelectronics class. Wire an LED to an Arduino UNO with current-limiting protection. Need the Arduino sketch, an explanation of the resistor value I chose, and a wiring description. Just give me a complete solution please, I'm short on time.
>pause|1

>tutor|Pushing back on the shortcut. The course wiki has an LED-Basics page that covers exactly this lab. Its opening:\n\n  A light-emitting diode is a one-way semiconductor that turns electrical current into light. It is polarity-sensitive (only conducts one way), has a forward voltage drop that depends on color, and will burn out without a current-limiting resistor in series.\n\nIn your own words, what's special about an LED that means it needs a resistor?
>pause|2
>wiki|LED-Basics

>student|it would burn out, too much current
>pause|1

>tutor|Right — and you've intuited exactly the trap. The Forward-Voltage page in the wiki explains the mechanism:\n\n  Below V_f the diode is essentially open (almost no current). Above V_f the slope is nearly vertical: tiny voltage increases cause large current increases. Because that slope is so steep, you can't safely connect an LED directly to a voltage source above its V_f.\n\nThe same page lists V_f for a red LED: about 2 V. If the Arduino supplies 5 V and the LED uses 2 V across itself, how much voltage is left for the resistor to drop?
>pause|2
>wiki|Forward-Voltage

>student|3V, 5 minus 2
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly. The Current-Limiting-Resistor page in your wiki has the sizing formula:\n\n  R = (V_supply − V_f) / I_target\n\nThe page also explains: "A resistor in series adds a controlled voltage drop proportional to current (Ohm's law: V = IR). It absorbs the difference between the supply and V_f and pins the current at a safe value."\n\nLEDs in this kit are rated about 20 mA max — a safe target is 15 mA. You've got 3 V to drop and a target current. Apply the formula — what's R?
>pause|2
>wiki|Current-Limiting-Resistor

>student|3 / 0.015 = 200 ohms
>pause|1

>tutor|Good math. The Current-Limiting-Resistor page in your wiki has a sizing table — "closest in kit" column — for each LED color. In practice, resistors come only in standard values (100, 150, 220, 330, etc.) and 200 isn't one. Which standard value should you pick, and why?
>pause|2

>student|220, higher is safer
>pause|1

>tutor|Right — 220 ohms gives about 13.6 mA, well under the 20 mA max, and the brightness difference is invisible. Resistor sized: done.\n\nNow the sketch. The Blink-Pattern page in your wiki opens with:\n\n  The "hello world" of microcontrollers — drive a digital pin HIGH for some interval, LOW for some interval, forever.\n\nYou'll write two functions: setup() runs once at boot, loop() runs forever after. The functions you'll need are pinMode(), digitalWrite(), and delay(). Pick a pin — D13 has the on-board LED so you can test without external wiring first. Paste your sketch when ready.
>pause|2
>wiki|Blink-Pattern

>student|Here:\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(13, INPUT);\n}\nvoid loop() {\n  digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\n  delay(1000);\n  digitalWrite(13, LOW);\n  delay(1000);\n}
>pause|1

>tutor|Hold on — before you upload, look at your setup() line. The pinMode-Setup page in your wiki defines the two modes:\n\n  OUTPUT — Pin drives current — sources or sinks up to ~20 mA, used for LEDs, buzzers, signal lines.\n  INPUT — Pin reads voltage — high impedance, requires an external pull-up or pull-down to avoid floating.\n\nYou want pin 13 to drive an LED. Which mode does the wiki say is right, and which did you write?
>pause|2
>wiki|pinMode-Setup

>student|OUTPUT, I wrote INPUT — let me fix
>pause|1

>tutor|Good — fix that and the LED will actually blink. Now the wiring. The LED-Basics page in your wiki covers polarity:\n\n  Longer leg = anode (+) — connects toward the positive supply (e.g., an Arduino digital output pin set HIGH).\n  Shorter leg + flat side of the plastic dome = cathode (−) — connects toward ground.\n\nDescribe how you'd wire it: where does each LED leg connect, and where does the resistor go?
>pause|2
>wiki|LED-Basics

>student|Longer leg to 5v, Shorter leg + flat side of the plastic dome to Arduino
>pause|1

>tutor|Hold on — two issues to revisit. First, you didn't mention the 220 ohm resistor anywhere — where does it go? Second, the wiki quote was "anode connects toward an Arduino digital output pin set HIGH," but you connected the anode to 5V (the always-on supply rail), not to D13 — the pin your sketch is actually toggling. Which pin should the anode connect to, and where does the resistor sit?
>pause|2

>student|oh right, anode to D13 through the 220, cathode to GND. Would this have worked if I had anode to 5v and cathode via the resistor to D13?
>pause|1

>tutor|Correct wiring — that's the standard "active-high" setup: D13 HIGH = LED on, D13 LOW = LED off.\n\nTo your question — yes, anode-to-5V and cathode-via-resistor-to-D13 would work, but with inverted logic. The Common-Anode-vs-Common-Cathode page in your wiki describes exactly this for RGB LEDs:\n\n  Drive a channel LOW → that color lights up. analogWrite(pin, 0) = full bright, 255 = off. Logic is inverted and trips students up.\n\nSame principle for a single LED. With your sketch unchanged, the LED would still blink — just inverted: ON during digitalWrite(13, LOW), OFF during digitalWrite(13, HIGH). The circuit physics is identical; only the timing relative to your code's intent flips.
>pause|2
>wiki|Common-Anode-vs-Common-Cathode

>student|ok that ends the session, wrap it up
>pause|1

>tutor|That's Lab 1 done — resistor sized with reasoning, sketch debugged (you caught the INPUT/OUTPUT slip), wiring correct, and you've got the active-low variant tucked in your back pocket. Good lab.
